  "query_preview": "..\n\n850-700mb moisture gradient collocated with general midlevel \nstretching deformation served as a focus for midlevel altocumulus \ncongestus development during the late morning hours along a line \nfrom Manchester to Harbor Beach. A secondary diffuse surface based \nwarm front is also lifting northward out of Ohio into portions of \nSoutheast Michigan for the early afternoon. Residual convective \ninhibition off of the high pressure from recent days is forecasted \nto hold in some fashion between 4.0-10.0 kft agl. So there will be \nsome background deterrent for upward motion due to the capping \ninversion. Poor model signal exists but 75-90th percentile of NBM 12 \nhour QPF progs does suggest some sporadic, isolated shower activity \nthis afternoon. Do think that the low potential for a thunderstorm \nwill come after 20z south of the I 69 corridor along the surface \nbased front. Daytime heating with the richer boundary layer moisture \nsuggests MLCAPE of around 1000 J/kg will be possible with 0-6km bulk \nshear of 25 knots or less. No severe weather is anticipated.  \n\nSurface cold front and dewpoint gradient will begin a journey \nsouthward tonight that will slow down in vicinity of Southeast \nMichigan. Models suggests a backdoor cold front working southward \novernight before stalling in the Thumb. Complex setup for early \nSunday with midlevel ridging component overhead as surface ridging \ntries to expand eastward. Composite trough axis with shortwave then \ndigs into Southeast Michigan but really appears to be out of phase \nwith any of the favorable dynamics of the upper level jet streak. \nLow chance of showers and/or rumble of thunder appears inevitable \nSunday afternoon but will likely require true absolute vorticity \nadvection. As a result, precipitation chances Sunday look to hold \noff until after 20z with some very modest midlevel fgen. \n\nMuch less humid air will be ushered into Southeast Michigan with \ncooler temperatures for Monday. Strong surface high pressure is \nprogged so any instability type shower potential should be highly \nlimited to any lake effect activity. Clear skies with highs around \n70 degrees.\n\nSurface high pressure will remain very progressive tracking out of \nLower Michigan by Tuesday. Models show significant warm air and \ndewpoint advection for Tuesday with highs climbing back above 80. \nDetails need to be ironed out yet but greatest dynamic support for \nascent appears likely for northern Wisconsin and the U.P. associated \nwith strong upper level jet forcing. Dipole subsidence under strong \nextension to upper level ridge may hold for a time Tuesday before a \nfar reaching baroclinic/frontal zone sags southward into the \nnorthern forecast area. A wet period then appears in store with \nconvection possible/persisting along the edge of the instability \nreservoir. There is a model signal that suggests a wave of \nfrontogenesis and low pressure will work through the northern Great \nLakes region late Wednesday and Wednesday night. Will need to \nmonitor for possible thunderstorm/MCS potential.",
